#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b8588f-11e5-563f-aadc-24d51197f6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358966} {:answer/id #uuid "20170f73-5100-5208-ac5f-751243af6d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358967} {:answer/id #uuid "cd216358-09d4-54b9-b7d1-89bd2d6832ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358968} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd5eed7-c3f9-5fe2-b8c3-ec5c993a60cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358969} {:answer/id #uuid "52746fa9-7504-5798-bf0a-0bbde116c921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd216358-09d4-54b9-b7d1-89bd2d6832ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358968} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2eb4fc-d879-5b42-8290-735e08416e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73ac4d85-91d1-5ad8-80cf-9b98d19a5316" :question/math-academy-id "q-113169" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea820507-e4a6-550e-80b5-4ae6cde10644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358973} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bb9674-11b4-5ee4-852b-d4ad672aa4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358974} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9faeb4-78a2-5681-9eb0-bf6ae3d7e636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358975} {:answer/id #uuid "f1edc508-eac2-5d96-b5f5-647e52f9f349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358976} {:answer/id #uuid "525be846-766c-5b21-9429-2c30da1a92ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "525be846-766c-5b21-9429-2c30da1a92ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358977} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10d0f32-219d-5f98-a5f3-4582f493a66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b21ee535-539b-5078-9c48-3b1c819d693a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113173" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bbe0a2-f33b-55d4-abe8-f5d3880e79a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358980} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb24a27-f25c-5c92-a2b4-85eba6f7ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358981} {:answer/id #uuid "4dab9c13-38a6-55de-981e-ff634b08035d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358982} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2429b2-2299-5938-8580-2980ac815d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358983} {:answer/id #uuid "484feb8e-ebc4-5f36-a0a8-cb3ab1b50c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb24a27-f25c-5c92-a2b4-85eba6f7ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358981} :answer-field/id #uuid "de09a269-0bd3-5614-a91c-ea5aa354b65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6076d1df-0a51-5be4-8eb1-e4f9aaadd8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113176" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{6}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11caacb-9c94-5c2f-8330-afdecfd4f3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358987} {:answer/id #uuid "77e3c3ca-6091-534d-807a-ef63a7afd5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358988} {:answer/id #uuid "e07d0efd-4392-5fbe-8773-138a80e79e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358989} {:answer/id #uuid "df074941-0dd3-5341-afb9-6108b2add7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358990} {:answer/id #uuid "654adcc5-319f-5657-bbd7-402ad3f4a0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e07d0efd-4392-5fbe-8773-138a80e79e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358989} :answer-field/id #uuid "48303d7a-e3f6-5cf4-a829-19a687ee1171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46cdbc89-38e8-5e50-824f-ae7d2846257a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113183" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{8{x}^{2}+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14574f5d-9fef-5e0a-84d3-03e5ad3b5644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358994} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6efa0d-ca66-5d3f-a810-292a58fcf623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358995} {:answer/id #uuid "464da4c9-214a-5940-ab57-8174ac122774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358996} {:answer/id #uuid "ade529c8-7f3a-57e1-8368-45d8f6ac77de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358997} {:answer/id #uuid "722bff88-a67b-57b1-9c05-91136b424675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14574f5d-9fef-5e0a-84d3-03e5ad3b5644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358994} :answer-field/id #uuid "c29a19c0-97cf-5dd4-8b8c-375d038c2334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "625f75a2-b61e-5217-82ce-3d541a7f692b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4636" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x+6}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x+6}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2x+1+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x+1+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{1} \\\\  & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}]}
